  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "0.14.0 — MINOR on the 0.x line, which is also the 0.x BREAKING signal.\n\nSix engine commits since 0.13.0. None touches the wire surface (src/FS.GG.Coord.Core/Protocol.fs), so\nthe exit codes and verb contract this repo's skills document are the ones this build enforces.\n\nTHE REASON TO TAKE THIS RELEASE: three of the six are fixes to the CLAIM PROTOCOL, found in production\non 2026-07-27 when seven workers ran concurrently against it. Every worker vendors `scripts/fsgg-coord`,\nwhich resolves and execs this package, so a receiver on 0.13.0 is running the protocol without them.\n\nBREAKING: a session is not a credential (.github#1646). `verifyHeld` granted `Held` when the claim\nmarker's id matched the id `--worker` NAMED and the two sessions were not known-different. Subagents\nshare a session id, so an impersonator satisfied both legs and could act on a lock they did not hold.\nThe refusal is enforced in the ENGINE at both of `Held`'s doors — `verifyHeld` and `claim` — so every\nverb that takes the capability is covered by construction: `release`, `heartbeat`, `widen`, `done`'s\nlock-drop, and the ADR-0041 chore lock. A call 0.13.0 accepted now exits non-zero.\n\n  $ fsgg-coord-engine claim .github#123 --worker someone-else   # 0.13.0: may succeed\n                                                                # 0.14.0: refused\n\nWHO THIS BREAKS: anything that passed a `--worker` it does not own — which was never a supported act,\nbut was reachable. The remedy is to pass your OWN minted id (`whoami --mint`), or `--force` if you\nreally mean to steal, which is loud and posts the theft.\n\nALSO in the same fix: `claim --force --worker THEM` used to destroy a third party's lock and sign the\ntheft notice with the INNOCENT worker's id, falsifying the accounting rather than merely bypassing it.\nThe notice now names the actor.\n\nBREAKING for consumers of scheduling ORDER: the blocking count ranks over the WHOLE board, not the\n--repo-scoped candidate set (.github#1628). `batch` and `next` can therefore choose a different item\nfor the same board than 0.13.0 would. The ranking is more correct — a row blocking ten items across\nthree repos outranks one blocking two in this repo — but it is an observable change for any caller that\ndepended on the previous pick.\n\nNEW PROJECTION: `reconcile --apply --json` now PARSES (.github#1541). 0.13.0 rejected the combination,\nso the org's one mutating reconcile verb had no machine projection at all and callers scraped prose.\n\nFIXES:\n\n  * The chore offer's board read is FRESH (.github#1679). It read the board through the 90-second\n    scheduling cache, so a CLASS-PROJECTION-LAG chore survived the very write that discharged it and\n    was offered again to the next worker. A signal that lies, in a fabric where workers are told to\n    trust signals. Fixed with a third ReadIntent rather than by borrowing Reconciling's warrant.\n  * BLOCKER-CLEARED respects the human-park sentinel it would overwrite (.github#1644). A row carrying\n    `Blocked on: human/...` was promoted to Ready by the very chore that is supposed to honour it.\n  * KitDigest hashes TWO runtime skill roots, not three (.github#1636) — .codex/skills is retired\n    (ADR-0065 as amended). This changes the computed digest for the same input tree.",
